Java candidate parity
=====================

Run from the repository root with GNU Make and Python 3.10+:

    make parity-java CANDIDATE=./run-interest CHAIN=interest CASE=i02-tie-half-cent
    make parity-java CANDIDATE='java -jar path/to/runner.jar' CHAIN=posting STEPS=POSTTRAN

Omit CASE to run every case in CHAIN; omit both to run all chains. CANDIDATE is
split into arguments (with shell-style quoting, but no shell evaluation).
The runner invokes it once per case, from the repository root, appending:

    --chain <name> --case <name> --input <absolute fixture input directory>
    --out <absolute output directory> --clock <frozen chain clock as one argument>

The candidate manages its own runtime and database setup. It must exit zero
when it successfully writes its outputs, even if a JCL return code is nonzero.
The runner clears work/parity-java/<chain>/<case>/actual before each invocation.
Write the same relative filenames and formats as the reference outputs:

    datasets/<DSN>.txt          One record per line; GDGs include G####V##.
    sysout/<JOB>.<STEP>.txt     Operator messages, one line each.
    rc.json                    Job names mapped to {"maxrc": 0, "steps": {"STEP": 0}}.
    db/<schema.table>.csv       Header and rows in the recorded order.

Use the fixture's expected/manifest.json as the reference for dataset layouts;
the candidate does not need to emit a manifest. Every selected golden output,
including an empty file, must be present. The runner writes its report to
work/parity-java/report.md and exits nonzero if any command or comparison fails.
It does not call the legacy recorder or require Docker.

Step-scoped comparison
----------------------

Use STEPS="POSTTRAN.STEP15 TRANREPT" with make, or compare an existing output:

    python3 tools/parity/compare.py posting p01-baseline-mixed \
        --actual work/parity-java/posting/p01-baseline-mixed/actual \
        --label java --steps POSTTRAN.STEP15

JOB selects its outputs and entire return-code entry. JOB.STEP selects only
that step's outputs and its return code, not the job-level maxrc. Extra
artifacts outside the scope are ignored. Dataset and DB snapshot ownership is
declared in compare.py; SYSOUT belongs to the job and step in its filename.
Unknown selectors or snapshots without an owner are errors in scoped mode.
Omit STEPS/--steps to compare the full chain as make parity does.

Harness self-test (not proof of a migration)
-------------------------------------------

This stand-in copies the goldens, then demonstrates that dropping one SYSOUT
fails full comparison but passes when comparing only POSTTRAN. Run from root:

    set -eu
    stub=$(mktemp)
    trap 'rm -f "$stub"' EXIT
    cat > "$stub" <<'PY'
    import argparse
    import shutil
    from pathlib import Path

    parser = argparse.ArgumentParser()
    for option in ("chain", "case", "input", "out", "clock"):
        parser.add_argument(f"--{option}", required=True)
    parser.add_argument("--drop", action="store_true")
    args = parser.parse_args()
    actual = Path(args.out)
    shutil.copytree(Path(args.input).with_name("expected"), actual, dirs_exist_ok=True)
    if args.drop:
        (actual / "sysout/TRANREPT.STEP10R.txt").unlink()
    PY

    make parity-java CANDIDATE="python3 $stub"  # all 24 cases pass
    if make parity-java CHAIN=posting CASE=p01-baseline-mixed CANDIDATE="python3 $stub --drop"; then
        echo "missing SYSOUT unexpectedly passed" >&2
        exit 1
    fi
    grep -F 'SYSOUT TRANREPT.STEP10R | `(file)` | present | missing' work/parity-java/report.md
    make parity-java CHAIN=posting CASE=p01-baseline-mixed STEPS=POSTTRAN CANDIDATE="python3 $stub --drop"
